Build fixes for cross-compiling NASM 3.00 to wasm32 with Emscripten.

Both hunks only ever matter off x86: a native build takes the X86_MEMORY
branch in bytesex.h, so neither line below is compiled and neither problem
is visible upstream.

1. include/bytesex.h - getu32() calls l32toh(), which is not defined
   anywhere in NASM or in any libc; the name is a typo for le32toh(). The
   two siblings on either side of it (getu16, getu64) call le16toh and
   le64toh, so the intent is unambiguous. This is an upstream bug and the
   fix is worth sending to the NASM maintainers.

2. include/compiler.h - wasm32 is little-endian and permits unaligned
   loads and stores on every memory instruction (the alignment immediate
   is a hint, not a constraint), which is exactly the contract X86_MEMORY
   describes. Claiming it lets NASM use direct unaligned accesses instead
   of the packed-struct fallback, and sidesteps hunk 1 in the bargain.

--- a/include/bytesex.h
+++ b/include/bytesex.h
@@ -215,7 +215,7 @@
 } __attribute__((packed));
 static inline uint32_t getu32(const void *p)
 {
-    return l32toh(((const struct unaligned32 *)p)->v);
+    return le32toh(((const struct unaligned32 *)p)->v);
 }
 static inline uint32_t setu32(void *p, uint32_t v)
 {
@@ -253,7 +253,7 @@
 static inline uint32_t getu32(const void *p)
 {
     const uint32_t _unaligned *pp = p;
-    return l32toh(*pp);
+    return le32toh(*pp);
 }
 static inline uint32_t setu32(void *p, uint32_t v)
 {
--- a/include/compiler.h
+++ b/include/compiler.h
@@ -147,7 +147,7 @@
  * is safe to leave it defined to 0 even if that is true.
  */
 #if defined(__386__) || defined(__i386__) || defined(__x86_64__) \
-    || defined(_M_IX86) || defined(_M_X64)
+    || defined(_M_IX86) || defined(_M_X64) || defined(__wasm__)
 # define X86_MEMORY 1
 # undef WORDS_BIGENDIAN
 # undef WORDS_LITTLEENDIAN
